  <h2>The three things that decide most disputed evaluations</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>How the daily loss limit resets.</strong> Server time, a specific hour, or calendar day? This single detail causes more disputed rule violations than anything else. Get it in writing before you start.</li>
    <li><strong>What counts as a restricted strategy.</strong> News trading, weekend holding, EAs, hedging, copy trading — restrictions vary by firm, and a strategy that's fine at one firm can fail you instantly at another.</li>
    <li><strong>What the payout process actually requires.</strong> Minimum amounts, waiting periods, and verification steps are often only fully described in the terms document, not the marketing page.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why we haven't ranked specific firms</h2>
  <p>Firm-by-firm comparisons are only worth publishing if someone has actually taken the evaluations and tested the payout process. Anything short of that is repeating marketing copy. We're not going to rank firms on commission, so this section stays as a framework until we've done that research properly.</p>
  <p>In the meantime, the most useful thing you can do is search any firm's name alongside "payout" or "withdrawal problem" and read what actual traders report — independent accounts are worth more than any firm's own testimonials page, including any list we could publish today.</p>

  <p class="meta">Prop firm evaluation fees are generally non-refundable, and most attempts across the industry do not pass. Treat the fee as a real cost you may not recover. Nothing on this page is investment advice.</p>
